{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner answers the four questions of scope contract revision 20 (9dbd3039, builder's request 22fc1a93, invitation e0721ecc). None blocks the review. (1) Counts too low: yes, the draw is a fault, the outcome stays offered and nothing is forced to an end state. A definition whose counts are too low is a definition error, and an invented end would be an outcome no rule derived. Two additions: the fault is shown as a state the member can see at the lane (plan 016's 'publication in progress' and 'effect unknown' distinctions), not only reported to operators; and the way out is the next revision of the definition or an operator's act, which the note should name. (2) The first compromised notice must be recordable at a full destination; it is the one message that may never wait for room. Decide R1-68 by its first, smaller option (settles gains a form for a truth value the entry sets, reserving the entry while the mark is unset) in the next revision, and the third milestone may build the destination to that form ahead of the text. (3) A refused withdraw waiting for room is acceptable until the next revision, not for good. A member's withdraw of their own pending request settles that request and should take its room from what the request held, so it never waits; only a withdraw that is itself refused as malformed is new work. The next revision states that. (4) Confirmed: an entry's hash may appear in a public ref name as its 64 lower-case hexadecimal characters with no prefix, the other parts of the name fixed ASCII, the whole name within Git's ref-name rules."}